		(at 30.48 136 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "91c7c3aa-5734-479f-ad75-ee0831294a66")
	)
	(text "Ported from PALTA pcb_design/supervisor.sch (Monitor logic, rev 0.2) - SPEC.md 9.2 / DECISIONS.md D008\nLogic (all 3.3 V):\n  GATED_HS = HS AND NAND(HS,LS);  GATED_LS = LS AND NAND(HS,LS)  ->  both low if HS,LS overlap\n  OC latch: 74AUP1G74, D=1, CLK = NOT(OC_TRIP) (NAND U1D, spare input tied high via R8)\n  Q latches on OC edge -> OC_LATCHED (MCU status);  /Q low -> OCP LED + reset chain + FAULT_BKIN;  cleared only by FAULT_RESET (act. low)\n  DRV_RST_OUT = DRV_RST_IN AND OC_LATCH_N            <-- CORRECTED, see CR-1 below\n  FAULT_BKIN = driver-fault line (act. low, 10k pull-up) OR'd with the OC latch through D904; RC 22R/220pF + BAT54S clamp -> MCU TIM1_BKIN (PB12)\n\nCR-1 (CRITICAL, fixed here). As ported, DRV_RST_OUT = DRV_RST_IN AND FAULT_BKIN AND OC_LATCH_N, and DRV_RST_OUT drives RDYC_BUS. The 1ED3491 clears FLT_N only on a\n  RISING edge of RDYC (ds 4.1 / 4.3.2.2), but RDYC could only rise once FLT_N was already high - a closed loop. After ANY DESAT event, driver over-temperature or\n  staggered power-up the gate drivers would have been latched off permanently, with no firmware or hardware path back. FAULT_BKIN has been removed from the AND chain\n  (U903 gate 3 is now spare) so a fault no longer holds its own reset low.\n  A power-on reset was added so the rising edge is guaranteed without firmware: C907 220 nF against R914 4.7k = tau 1.03 ms; DRV_RST_IN crosses the ALVC08 VIH (2.0 V)\n  at 1.03 ms x ln(3.3/1.3) = 0.96 ms, comfortably past the drivers' ~200 us t_START and the 1.0-1.2 us t_CLRMIN. MCU-driven reset pulses are slewed to ~90 us, still\n  far longer than t_CLRMIN.\nMN-1: C906 100 nF on FAULT_RESET against R913 4.7k = 470 us holds the OC latch CLEARED through power-up (its Q was previously indeterminate and could hold RDYC low,\n  making the board look dead). 470 us < 960 us, so the latch is released before the driver reset is - the ordering is deliberate.\nMJ-1: OC_LATCH_N now reaches TIM1_BKIN through D904 (BAT54WS). Before this the 300 A hardware trip only reached the driver-reset chain, so SPEC 9.2's central claim -\n  that overcurrent tri-states the PWM in silicon - was false as built. Polarity stays active-low and the diode blocks back-feed from a driver-only fault.\nMJ-13: R911/R912 were 100R, drawing ~11 mA per LED. With the fault LED on FAULT_BKIN that current back-fed through R910 (22R) into DRV_FAULT_IN; the FLT_N pins are\n  specified at 300 mV / 5 mA, so the wire-OR sat near 0.94 V against a 0.99 V logic threshold - the fault was barely readable. Now 2.2k (~0.6 mA per LED) and the\n  driver-fault LED sits on DRV_FAULT_IN ahead of R910, so FAULT_BKIN carries no LED current at all and follows the wire-OR to ~0.1 V.\n\nLabel mapping vs original:\n  PWM_UT/VT/WT -> PWM_HS_1/2/3        PWM_UB/VB/WB -> PWM_LS_1/2/3\n  PWM_GUT..GWB -> PWM_HS/LS_x_GATED   ~OC_Fault_U/V/W (wired-OR) -> OC_TRIP (single 300 A comparator, SPEC 3.3)\n  OC_Fault -> OC_LATCHED    CLR_OC_Fault -> FAULT_RESET    ~FAULT_IN -> DRV_FAULT_IN    ~FAULT -> FAULT_BKIN\n  ~DRIVER_RST_IN -> DRV_RST_IN    ~DRIVER_RST_OUT -> DRV_RST_OUT\n\nDeviations (documented, see report):\n  - Single OC_TRIP input replaces 3 wired-OR per-phase comparators (300 A trip lives with current sensing).\n  - AND gates: original BOM fitted SN74ACT08 at +5 V; here SN74ALVC08 at 3.3 V (all-3.3 V design).\n  - No hardware dead-time insertion here: original only eliminates overlap (SPEC 9.2 row 2 remains with gate drivers).\n  - R913/R914 4.7k pull-ups on FAULT_RESET / DRV_RST_IN (not in original): keeps latch-clear and driver-reset inactive (high) while the MCU pins are Hi-Z during boot.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 137.16 160.02 0)
